-- title:
--   j and j_q lie in the integral closure of A₀[j]
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (realised as `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`) and an intermediate field $K$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$. Work inside the Laurent series field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$, where `jqModC` denotes the $q$-expansion $t^{-1}\cdot(E_4^3\,\eta^{-24})$ of the modular invariant $j$, with integral coefficients pushed into $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and `jqNModC` at level $N$ denotes its image under the ring homomorphism `qExpand` that multiplies all exponents by $N$, i.e. the series $j_N$ obtained by substituting $t\mapsto t^{N}$. Let `jRing` $A\,K$ be the subring generated by the constant series with coefficients in $A\cap K$ together with `jqModC`, let `fieldOver` $N\,K$ be the subfield generated by the constant series with coefficients in $K$ together with `jqModC` and `jqNModC` at level $N$, and let `jIntegralClosure` $N\,A\,K$ be the subring of those elements of `fieldOver` $N\,K$ that are integral over `jRing` $A\,K$. The assertion is that, for $N = 1\cdot q$, both `jqModC` and `jqNModC` at level $1\cdot q$ belong to `jIntegralClosure` $(1\cdot q)\,A\,K$.
--
--   This records the integrality of the two standard generators $j$ and $j_q$ over the ring $(A\cap K)[j]$, the source of integrality being the modular equation $\Phi_q(j,Y)$. It supplies the two membership hypotheses used by the classification of height-one primes in the node-localised setting, and is cited by the statements there about the residues of $\sigma(j)$ and $\sigma(j_q)$ at centred sections of the crossing parameter.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_NodeLocalized_jqModC_mem_jIntegralClosure_and_jqNModC_mem.lean

